---
import Plain from '@/layouts/Plain.astro';

/** Terms, written plainly: what this is, what it is not, and what it costs (nothing). */
const section = 'mt-6 space-y-2';
const heading = 'text-base font-semibold';
const list = 'list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm';
---

<Plain title="Terms — noka" theme={Astro.locals.theme}>
  <h1 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Terms</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">Short, because the product is small.</p>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>1. The service</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      noka stores emergency contacts and prints them on a card with a QR code and a six-digit PIN.
      Anyone holding the card who knows the PIN can read those contacts. That is the entire point,
      and it is also the risk you accept by using it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>2. Not an emergency service, and not a monitored one</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      noka is not a substitute for emergency services, medical advice or a written emergency plan,
      and it is not a medical device. It is a more elegant way to keep a note in your wallet. Call
      your local emergency number first; this card is for the people you would want reached
      afterwards.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Nothing here is monitored. No one is watching for a scan, opening a card sends no alert, and a
      card that is never found or never opened raises nothing at all. The owner can see that a card
      was opened — that is the whole of it, and it is not a distress signal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>3. The printed card</h2>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>
        The card is a convenience, not a safety device, and it is only as good as the paper it is
        printed on and the place it is kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        It shows whatever the account holds at the moment someone opens it, so editing a contact
        changes every card already printed — no card needs reprinting for that.
      </li>
      <li>
        It stops working the moment the card is replaced or the account is deleted. Nobody holding
        the printed copy is told; replacing a card means destroying the old one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>4. Your account</h2>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>You are responsible for your password, and for keeping the printed card somewhere sensible.</li>
      <li>Anyone with the card and the PIN can read the contacts on it — that is the design, not a flaw.</li>
      <li>Making a new card invalidates the old link and PIN immediately; printing a fresh one is your responsibility.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>5. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Do not put data in a contact or note that you are not allowed to hold — someone else's
      medical details, for instance. Do not use the service to harass anyone. Do not attempt to
      access cards that are not yours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>6. Price</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      It is free, and it stays free: there is no tier, no trial and no plan that turns the card off.
      Sponsors, when there are any, appear as static logos on the landing and sign-in pages — never
      on a card page, and never with a pixel, a counter or any data about you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>7. Availability and liability</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      The service is provided as it is, without warranty. It may be unavailable, and a card might
      fail to open at the worst possible moment. To the extent the law allows, the operator is not
      liable for indirect or consequential loss arising from its use. Nothing here limits liability
      that cannot be limited by law.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>8. Source and license</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      The code is MIT licensed: you may self-host, fork and change it, including commercially. The
      license binds anyone who takes the code, including the operator.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>9. Changes</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      These terms will change as the service does. Material changes will be noted in the
      repository's changelog, which is public.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-8 border-t border-line pt-4 text-xs text-fg-muted">
    This page describes the current build and is not legal advice.
  </p>
</Plain>
